summer fun is here but we can't forget  summer dangers intermountain healthcare  the sponsors of this next segment remind  us how to take care of our elderly  community in the heat  [Music]  my name is dr audrey sue cruz i am an  internal medicine physician here at  intermountain mygen  clinic we specialize in patients who are  65 and older the senior population we  also see younger patients as well  so i work at the blue diamond clinic  which is where we are right now and we  service the southwest area of las vegas  so it's getting really hot now during  the summer time and of course every year  it gets really really hot here in las  vegas and what we want our patients to  know especially in the senior population  is how to keep themselves safe during  this really difficult very hot time in  las vegas so now as the temperatures are  starting to rise over 100 degrees we  want our patients to know  the things to look out for in terms of  dehydration and heat related illnesses  things to look out for for dehydration  include really high and fast heartbeat  feeling dry sweating profusely  having muscle cramps and overall just  not feeling well and things to avoid are  staying outside for long periods of time  we definitely want our patients to carry  around water bottles with them at all  times  and you don't need to wait until you're  thirsty to drink water definitely if  it's you're already thirsty that means  it might be a bit too late so definitely  keep drinking water throughout the day  find yourself shade when you are outside  try not to be outside for long periods  of time and then also find cooling  centers in the community where you can  sit inside and cool down so libraries  shopping malls grocery stores things  like that where you can make sure that  your body temperature  can decrease a little bit and in terms  of drinking water a really good  recommendation is drinking half your  body weight in ounces of fluid so for  example if someone were  to weigh 100 pounds we would recommend  that they drink at least 50 ounces of  fluid per day we also recommend eating a  proper diet lots of fruits and veggies  and also avoiding too much strenuous  activity especially during the summer we  still want our patients to exercise but  definitely try to avoid too much  strenuous activity  outside so there are several types of  heat related illnesses including mild  issues like heat cramps or heat rash but  there are more extreme cases too that  include heat exhaustion and even heat  stroke which can be fatal so some  symptoms for heat exhaustion and heat  stroke can include heavy sweating  cold pale and clammy skin  muscle cramps dizziness fast weak pulse  and even nausea and vomiting so if a  patient has any of these symptoms it's  important to rest in a cool shaded  location and drink plenty of water  a more severe symptom of heat stroke is  a body temperature of 103 degrees and  higher and in that cases a person should  cool down very quickly either in an ice  bath or using ice packs and they should  seek medical attention so one tip that  we have is to try to do any physical  activities away from concrete or  artificial turf these surfaces tend to  soak up more of the sun's energy and can  increase the heat in any area by several  degrees we encourage people to plan  ahead and know that in temperatures like  these people may have to deviate from  their normal routine to avoid  dehydration and heat illness if you have  any questions at all about heat-related  illness or how to keep yourself safe  please feel free to talk to your  physician we here at intermountain mygen  clinic are here for you come see us  and i just wanted to remind everyone in  the community to please check in on your  elderly family members elderly neighbors  and elderly friends  thanks again to intermountain healthcare  for sponsoring this segment for more  information visit the website on your  screen  intermountainhealthcare.org slash nevada  we'll be right
 

